{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 26, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the twelfth century. His life story involves complex political maneuvers and military conflicts that shaped the history of the Crusader states in the Levant. He was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem which gave him strong royal connections through his mother who was the sister of King Baldwin III. This lineage placed him at the center of power struggles following the death of King Amalric I when the throne passed to the young and leprous Baldwin IV.\n\n*Early Life and Succession Challenges*\nRaymond faced immediate threats upon inheriting his title because his father was assassinated by the Hashshashin order. He spent time as a captive in Aleppo before gaining freedom to rule his county. His intelligence and diplomatic skills allowed him to navigate the dangerous landscape of Frankish nobility while maintaining stability in Tripoli. He acted as regent for Baldwin IV during periods when the king was too ill to govern alone. This position gave him immense influence over royal policy and military strategy against rising Muslim powers under Saladin.\n\n*Political Factions and Internal Strife*\nThe kingdom split into two main factions during this era and Raymond led the moderate group known as the Pullani. These nobles favored diplomacy and truces with Muslim neighbors rather than constant warfare. Opposing them were the aggressive Courtenay family members who sought immediate confrontation. Raymond married Eschiva of Bures to secure the Principality of Galilee and this alliance strengthened his political standing significantly. Tensions escalated when Guy of Lusignan married Sibylla and threatened to usurp control from the ailing king. Raymond opposed Guy fiercely due to concerns about his competence and rash decisions that could endanger the entire realm.\n\n*Final Years and Legacy*\nRaymond made a separate peace with Saladin in 1187 after falling out with King Guy which some contemporaries viewed as treasonous though he likely sought to preserve his lands. The disastrous Battle of Hattin occurred shortly thereafter and resulted in the destruction of the Crusader army along with the loss of Jerusalem itself. Raymond escaped the carnage and retreated to Tripoli where he died later that same year possibly from illness or despair over the catastrophic defeat. Historians debate his actions during these final months but most agree that his pragmatic approach represented the best chance for survival that the Crusader states possessed before their collapse. His death marked the end of an era defined by intricate balancing acts between Christian factions and Muslim sultanates.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -9.536789e-06}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.00272735}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.00013805389}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.009584976}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.5762793e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " until", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -2.2173173e-05}, {"token": " death", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -8.3446537e-07}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.3113031e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -8.3446537e-07}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -5.960466e-07}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.013017798}, {"token": " played", "logprob": -0.028189834}, {"token": " 